## ASSEMBLED SECTION



### <img src="https://flagcdn.com/ua.svg" width="32" alt="Ukraine" style={{display: 'inline', verticalAlign: 'middle', position: 'relative', top: '-2px', marginRight: '8px', border: '1px solid #d1d5db', borderRadius: '2px'}} />Ukraine

Ukraine maintains active diplomatic engagement while operating under timeline pressure, demonstrating continued agency in shaping peace process terms through high-level Munich Security Conference diplomacy and bilateral engagement with major powers including China. Military posture remains offensive with continued institutional development through second-stage corps reform and expanding deep-strike capabilities, while economic architecture functions stably within Western financing frameworks. The Budanov security professional leadership model continues with emerging political discussions on mobilization policy adjustments to address enforcement challenges.

**Key developments:**

- **Diplomatic:** Zelensky attended Munich Security Conference where Trump publicly told him to 'get moving' on peace negotiations, while Zelensky urged intensified pressure on Russia and pushed for more military aid including Patriot missiles and Tomahawks. Zelensky framed ending the war as Trump's potential legacy achievement and political victory.
- **Diplomatic:** Sybiha held multiple bilateral meetings with counterparts from China, EU, Canada and others, discussing peace efforts, security guarantees, sanctions, and Ukraine's EU membership path. Invited Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi to visit Ukraine, noting China as Ukraine's primary trading partner with $21 billion in bilateral trade.
- **Diplomatic:** Reports suggest Budanov has discussed with Zelensky's aides the legal and practical conditions under which Ukraine could withdraw from certain Donetsk region areas while preventing Russian troop entry. He is described as 'a skilled negotiator and proponent of peace.'
- **Security:** Commander-in-Chief Syrskyi announced the 'second stage' of corps reform, with each corps now having organic artillery brigades and unmanned systems battalions expanded to regiment size. Reported 74% air defense effectiveness and that increased enemy losses are a result of the reform.
- **Security:** Fedorov revealed one strategic objective was 'to kill 50,000 Russians a month,' exceeding the roughly 30,000-35,000 new troops Russia recruits monthly. Urged partners to deliver PAC-3 interceptor missiles and outlined clear task to 'make the cost of war for Russia unbearable.'

<Accordion title="Other Stories">
- **Zelensky faces pressure from Trump at Munich Conference, calls for more US support against Russia** — President Zelensky attended the Munich Security Conference where he urged Trump to intensify pressure on Russia and criticized being asked to make too many concessions. Trump publicly told Zelensky to 'get moving' on peace negotiations, while Zelensky pushed for more military aid including Patriot missiles and Tomahawks. *([politico.com](https://politico.com/news/2026/02/13/zelenskyy-pushes-us-to-intensify-pressure-on-russia-00780844))*
- **Foreign Minister Sybiha conducts extensive diplomatic meetings at Munich Security Conference** — Foreign Minister Andrii Sybiha held multiple bilateral meetings at the Munich Security Conference with counterparts from China, EU, Canada, and other countries. He discussed peace efforts, security guarantees, sanctions on Russia, and Ukraine's EU membership path. Sybiha also opened Ukraine House at the conference alongside German officials. *([ukrinform.net](https://euromaidanpress.com/2026/02/13/us-signals-readiness-to-ratify-ukraine-security-deal-in-congress))*
- **Ukraine and IMF agree to ease conditions on $8.2 billion loan program** — Prime Minister Yuliia Svyrydenko announced that Ukraine and the IMF have agreed to simplify conditions for a new $8.2 billion lending program, including easing sensitive tax increases. The agreement follows discussions where structural benchmarks were revised, and the program will be considered at the next IMF Executive Board meeting. *([reuters.com](https://www.reuters.com/business/ukraine-imf-ease-conditions-new-82-billion-loan-program-2026-02-14/))*
- **Head of Presidential Office Budanov addresses Ukrainian youth and discusses peace negotiations** — Kyrylo Budanov held an online meeting with representatives of Ukrainian youth organizations abroad from around 150 organizations across all continents. Reports suggest Budanov has been involved in discussions about legal and practical conditions for potential territorial withdrawals in the Donetsk region as part of peace negotiations. *([president.gov.ua](https://www.president.gov.ua/en/news/ukrayina-sogodni-ye-shitom-yevropi-kirilo-budanov-obgovoriv-102817))*
- **Prime Minister Svyrydenko announces US-Ukraine reconstruction fund progress and other initiatives** — Prime Minister Yuliia Svyrydenko announced that the US-Ukraine Reconstruction Investment Fund (URIF) received more than 60 project proposals in its first month. She also reported allocating UAH 231 million to complete school shelter construction in 11 regions and discussed consequences of Russian strikes on customs and tax revenues. *([interfax.com.ua](https://komersant.ua/en/ponad-60-propozytsiy-do-fondu-rekonstruktsii-ukrainy-ta-ssha-svyrydenko-povidomyla-detali/))*
- **Ukrainian parliament discusses mobilization policy and military service changes** — Ukrainian parliament members, particularly from Servant of the People party, discussed mobilization policy changes. MP Fedyr Venyslavskyi indicated mobilization procedures may change soon and suggested making life more difficult for those avoiding recruitment. The discussions touched on demobilization possibilities and mobilization resources. *([novyny.live](https://novyny.live/vijskova-ekonomika/demobilizatsiia-v-umovakh-voiennogo-stanu-shcho-kazhut-u-vru-312179.html))*
- **National Bank of Ukraine reports increase in international reserves** — The National Bank of Ukraine reported that net international reserves increased by 6.4% to $43.3 billion in December compared to the previous month. Official reserve assets increased by 4.6% to $57.292 billion, with annual growth of 30.8% from the previous year. *([ukranews.com](https://ukranews.com/en/news/1133461-the-nbu-s-net-international-reserves-increased-by-6-4-to-43-3-billion-in-december))*
- **NATO discussions and European security architecture developments** — Multiple reports discuss NATO developments including the concept of 'NATO 3.0' based on partnership rather than dependency, tensions with the US over European security burden-sharing, and NATO's assessment that Ukrainian defense is unlikely to collapse in the near term. Discussions also covered NATO exercises revealing vulnerabilities and potential Arctic missions. *([unian.ua](https://eurointegration.com.ua/news/2026/02/12/7231021))*
- **Servant of the People party political developments and analysis** — Coverage of the Servant of the People party included historical analysis of its evolution from a 2016 party to the current ruling party, discussions about its role in current mobilization policies, and various parliamentary activities. The coverage examined the party's transformation from a TV series-inspired political movement to Ukraine's governing party. *([kokl.ua](https://kokl.ua/partiya-sluga-narodu-vid-serialu-do-vlady/))*
</Accordion>


